# What Shall We Do with the Drunken Sailor, strum and sing (repertoire plan: drunken-sailor).
# A new arrangement of the traditional shanty, in E Dorian: E minor with C# instead of C.
# Your part: two chords, Em (022000) and D (xx0232; strum strings 4 to 1 only). Every line takes
# four bars of 2/4: Em under line 1, D under line 2, Em under line 3, then D and Em under the
# last line. First chorus: two downstrums a bar, beat 1 a little louder. Then down-up eighths.
# The tune (second track) as it is commonly sung, in first position on strings 4 to 1:
#   Way hay and up she rises:  B B B | B E G B, then A A A | A D F# A, then B B B | B C# D E,
#   Early in the morning:      D B A F# E E.
# The words of the verse follow the same notes. The plan allows the traditional chorus and the
# verse "Put him in the longboat till he's sober", so only those are sung here.
title: What Shall We Do with the Drunken Sailor
id: drunken-sailor
composer: Traditional (sea shanty)
source: Sailors' work song described in 1839, printed in shanty collections by the 1890s; arranged for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain (traditional); this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 2
tags: strumming, shanty, dorian, 2/4, two chords, lyrics
tempo: 96
time: 2/4
key: E Dorian

track: Your strum
role: rhythm
